Suit yourself.  If it were me, I'd put Ubuntu[1] on the new system
and let the old one keep its existing Windows install.  That avoids
the need to break in a new Windows system -- installing all your
applications and everything all over again, which can be quite time
consuming -- and it also means the system you'll inevitably end up
using most of the time will be the newer one.  (Once you get used
to using a Unix-type system, you'll find you have remarkably fewer
uses for a Windows system than you expected to have.  I speak from
personal experience here.  When I first experimented with multiboot,
back in 1998, I expected to use DOS and Windows 95 most of the time
and Linux on occasion, for learning purposes and such.  About a year
later I realized it had been months and months since I'd booted DOS
or Windows.  I was doing everything on the Linux system because it
was just easier.  Huh.  I didn't expect that, but there it was.)

Oh, and if you're geeky enough to buy parts and build the computer
yourself to your own specifications, putting Ubuntu on the new
system and leaving Windows on the old one avoids the need to buy
a new Windows license.  At system-builder prices, that's about a
hundred bucks, I think, depending on which version you get.  Spend
fifty of that on extra RAM or something and pocket the rest.

